.\" coscad(1) - CoScad compiler manual page (mdoc)
.Dd September 11, 2026
.Dt COSCAD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm coscad
.Nd glyph/pipeline CAD language compiler: .coscad and .assemble to OpenSCAD, STL, and print beds
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar part.coscad
.Op Fl o Ar part.scad
.Nm
.Cm validate
.Ar part.coscad | spec.assemble
.Nm
.Cm stl
.Ar part.coscad
.Op Fl o Ar part.stl
.Nm
.Ar spec.assemble
.Nm
.Cm next
.Op Fl -strict
.Ar spec.assemble
.Nm
.Cm check
.Ar spec.assemble
.Op Fl -keep-temp
.Nm
.Cm plan
.Ar spec.assemble
.Op Fl -png
.Nm
.Cm site
.Ar dir
.Ar spec.assemble ...
.Nm
.Cm doctor
.Nm
.Fl -version | Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Cm validate
checks a .coscad or .assemble file, including recursive part sources and
rigid mating constraints, without writing output files or rendering meshes.
It is suitable for editor diagnostics. It does not run interference or
instruction-access checks.
.Nm
compiles CoScad part files to OpenSCAD source and, with OpenSCAD and the
BOSL2 library installed, renders them to STL.
A
.Pa .coscad
file is a list of definitions, one per line; the definition named
.Ql main
is the rendered shape.
An
.Pa .assemble
file declares physically separate parts, how many to print, and how they
sit together; the manufacturing stage orients and packs them onto print
beds.
.Pp
Anchors, attachments, bezier outlines, lofts, fit checks, print orientation,
and bed packing are all computed in the compiler, so the emitted OpenSCAD
stays plain
.Pq translate , rotate , union , difference , BOSL2 primitives
and any expression, including composites, can be attached to again.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Nm Ar part.coscad Op Fl o Ar out.scad
Compile one part to OpenSCAD.
Writes
.Pa part.scad
next to the input unless
.Fl o
is given.
.It Nm Cm stl Ar part.coscad Op Fl o Ar out.stl
Compile and render to an ASCII STL through OpenSCAD, then print the triangle
count, volume, and bounding box.
The intermediate
.Pa .scad
is kept beside the STL.
.It Nm Ar spec.assemble
Design stage.
Emits the assembled view
.Pq Pa spec_asm.scad ,
one print-oriented
.Pa spec_part_<variant>.scad
per unique (part, orientation), the packed plate(s)
.Pq Pa spec_plate.scad , or Pa spec_plate1.scad , Pa spec_plate2.scad ... ,
and the design manifest
.Pa spec_design.json
(schema
.Li coscad.design/1 ) .
It never writes
.Pa spec_manifest.json ;
if it finds a design manifest left there by coscad 1.2 or earlier it prints a
note to standard error and leaves the file in place.
.It Nm Cm next Oo Fl -strict Oc Ar spec.assemble
Manufacturing stage.
Renders each variant once, chooses a print orientation where none was
declared (scoring bed contact, overhang, height, and slenderness on the real
mesh), packs instances largest-first across as many beds as needed, and
writes
.Pa spec_bed<N>.stl
plus
.Pa spec_manifest.json
(schema
.Li coscad.next/1 ) ,
recording per-variant orientation and printability and per-instance
placements for slice-once, stamp-many slicing.
Printability warnings go to standard error and to the manifest, each with a
code in
.Ql warning_codes
.Pq Ql small_contact , Ql tall_narrow , Ql flat_overhang , Ql elevated_start .
With
.Fl -strict ,
any warning makes
.Cm next
exit 2 after the outputs are written.
Instances with a brim (see
.Sx ASSEMBLIES )
are packed with the brim reserved around their footprint; each placement
records
.Ql brim_mm .
.It Nm Cm check Ar spec.assemble Op Fl -keep-temp
Interference and clearance check on real meshes.
Every part is rendered as placed by the
.Ql asm
expression; close pairs are intersected exactly by OpenSCAD.
Reports
.Ql OVERLAP
with its volume,
.Ql contact
for touching faces, and
.Ql WARN
for gaps below the declared
.Ql clearance .
Exit status 1 if any overlap.
Scratch renders go to the system temp directory and are removed unless
.Fl -keep-temp .
.It Nm Cm plan Ar spec.assemble Op Fl -png
Assembly instruction generator.
From the
.Ql asm
placement, part hints, and
.Ql fastener
lines, derives the requires-before graph (T-nuts before the slot they
need is covered, host and clamped part before each screw, slide-in nuts
before anything sealing a slot end), checks every step for support,
balance, driver access, and nut entry, and searches rest-face phases with
a cost table so the build needs few flips, rails lie flat while nuts are
started, and drivers stay vertical.
Writes
.Pa spec_plan.md
(bill of materials, T-nut preload sheet, numbered steps with torque per
host material),
.Pa spec_plan.json ,
and one
.Pa spec_stepN.scad
scene per step
.Pq this step orange, earlier parts grey, screw heads red, a grey bench slab ;
.Fl -png
also renders them through OpenSCAD: fully evaluated with the Manifold backend
(OpenSCAD 2025.05 or newer; no preview artefacts on mated faces, same speed),
or the preview on older builds, with a note.
Design errors (colliding screws, too little thread reach, a clamp not on
its host face) are reported and exit 2.
See
.Sx ASSEMBLIES .
.It Nm Cm site Ar dir Ar spec.assemble ...
Companion site: runs
.Cm plan
with renders for every assembly and writes a static, phone-first site
under
.Ar dir :
one page per build (step cards with images, rest face, parts, T-nut
preloads, screws with torque, warnings; prev/next navigation, filter
within the build) and an index that searches across builds by name,
part, material, or screw spec.
Plain HTML, no server needed: open
.Pa dir/index.html
from a file or host the folder anywhere.
.It Nm Cm doctor
Report the OpenSCAD binary and BOSL2 checkout that will be used, their
versions, and the result of an end-to-end test render against a known
volume.
Exit status 1 if anything is missing.
.It Fl -version , Fl -help
Print the version, or the command list with a language cheat sheet.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width COSCAD_OPENSCAD
.It Ev COSCAD_OPENSCAD
The OpenSCAD binary, or a wrapper script such as
.Ql xvfb-run -a openscad "$@"
on a headless machine.
Otherwise
.Ql openscad
on
.Ev PATH
is used, then the usual install locations
.Pq the macOS app bundle, Homebrew, /usr/bin, /usr/local/bin, snap .
.It Ev COSCAD_BOSL2
A BOSL2 checkout outside the OpenSCAD library folders; its parent is
prepended to
.Ev OPENSCADPATH
when OpenSCAD runs.
Otherwise the library folders are searched
.Pq Pa ~/Documents/OpenSCAD/libraries , Pa ~/.local/share/OpenSCAD/libraries , ... .
.It Ev COSCAD_OPENSCAD_TIMEOUT
Seconds a single OpenSCAD run may take before it is killed and reported as a
failure (default 600), so a stuck render cannot hang
.Cm stl ,
.Cm next ,
.Cm check ,
.Cm plan --png
or an unattended pipeline.
.El
.Sh LANGUAGE
One definition per line,
.Ql name = expression .
Definitions resolve by dependency, not order.
Comments start with
.Ql // .
A line beginning with
.Ql |>
continues the previous definition.
The first code line may select a vocabulary:
.Ql !glyph
(glyph operators),
.Ql !simple
(ASCII words such as
.Ql Box ,
.Ql Translate.x ,
.Ql Hull ,
.Ql *
for union and
.Ql -
for difference), or neither, which accepts both.
.Ss Numbers
A definition whose right-hand side is arithmetic is a numeric binding.
Numbers may reference numbers, in any order, and are usable wherever a
number goes.
Argument positions take one atom
.Pq a literal, a name, Ql -name , or a parenthesized expression
so juxtaposed arguments stay unambiguous:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
w = 20
t = w / 5 - 1
main = box w (w / 2) t |> cutat top 0 0 (-t / 2) (zcyl 1 50)
.Ed
.Ss Shapes
.Bl -column "▬ x y z / box x y z" "description" -offset indent -compact
.It Sy syntax Ta Sy shape
.It ■ s Ta cube, corner at the origin (OpenSCAD)
.It ▬ x y z Ta box, corner at the origin
.It ● r Ta sphere
.It ◎ r h Ta cylinder, z from 0 to h
.It ▻ r h Ta cone
.It ⎏ n r h Ta n-sided prism
.It cube s / box x y z Ta centered cuboid (BOSL2)
.It sphere r / cyl r h Ta centered sphere, cylinder
.It xcyl r l / ycyl / zcyl Ta axis-aligned centered cylinders
.It tube or ir h Ta centered tube
.It torus R r Ta torus
.It wedge x y z Ta right-triangular prism, profile in the YZ plane
.It ▣ x y z c Ta cuboid, chamfer c
.It ◙ x y z r Ta cuboid, rounding r (< half the smallest dimension)
.It ⌭ r h c / ⌽ r h r2 Ta cylinder with chamfered / rounded ends
.It ⊚ or ir h Ta tube
.It ⏢ x1 y1 x2 y2 h Ta prismoid
.It ◉ R r Ta torus
.It ⊿ x y z Ta wedge
.El
.Ss 2D profiles
.Ql △ r ,
.Ql ⬠ r ,
.Ql ⭘ r
(triangle, pentagon, circle) and
.Ql ✎ x0 y0 x1 y1 ...
(a closed piecewise-cubic bezier outline, 3k+1 control points, evaluated by
the compiler).
Extrude with
.Ql ⮕ h profile
or
.Ql |> extrude h ;
offset with
.Ql profile ↯ ● r .
A profile used where a solid is expected, or the reverse, is a compile
error.
.Ss Lofts
.Ql loft z0 p0 z1 p1 ...
(glyph
.Ql ⟰ ,
.Ql Loft
in !simple) skins 2D profiles at ascending z into one solid through BOSL2
.Fn skin .
Profiles may differ in vertex count.
.Ql p0 |> loft z1 p1
starts a loft with
.Ql p0
at z = 0; further
.Ql |> loft z p
stages append.
Profiles must be single closed outlines: the 2D primitives, beziers, and
their in-plane transforms
.Pq χ ψ ω ⬈ ⇋ ↯ ;
booleans between profiles, ζ, θ, and ϕ are rejected.
.Ss Booleans
.Ql ⊕
union,
.Ql ⊖
difference (left minus right),
.Ql ∩
intersection,
.Ql ⇓
hull,
.Ql ⊞
minkowski.
Left-associative, one precedence level.
.Ss Transforms
.Ql χ ψ ζ d
translate along x/y/z;
.Ql θ ϕ ω deg
rotate about x/y/z (about the origin);
.Ql ⬈ sx sy sz
scale;
.Ql ⇋ nx ny nz
mirror;
.Ql ⚓ anchor
re-origin a shape at one of its own anchors.
A transform applies to the next primary expression; use parentheses or
.Ql $
.Pq Ql χ 5 $ a ⊕ b
for a compound argument.
.Ss Pipelines and anchors
.Ql a |> stage |> stage ...
applies postfix stages:
.Ql x y z d ,
.Ql move dx dy dz ,
.Ql rotx roty rotz deg ,
.Ql scale ,
.Ql mirror ,
.Ql extrude h ,
.Ql loft z p ,
.Ql anchor v ,
.Ql add cut isect hull mink shape ,
and the relational stages
.Ql at v [dx dy dz] child
(union; the child's opposite anchor meets anchor v),
.Ql on v [dx dy dz] child
(union; child rotated so +Z points along v, bottom mated there), and
.Ql cutat v [dx dy dz] cutter
(difference; the cutter is centered at the anchor).
Anchors are bounding-box points of the shape so far:
.Ql top bot lft rt fwd bak ctr ,
combinable as
.Ql top+rt
or
.Ql lft+fwd .
Glyph forms:
.Ql a ⌖ v b
positions,
.Ql a ⋈ v b
attaches.
.Ss Names and keywords
Names are resolved by position.
Only the primitive shape words are
.Em reserved :
.Ql cube box sphere cyl tube torus wedge xcyl ycyl zcyl loft
in every mode and, outside
.Ql !glyph ,
the capitalised words
.Ql Sphere Cube Box Cylinder Cone Tube Torus Wedge Prismoid Circle Triangle Pentagon Bezier Translate Rotate Scale Mirror Extrude Loft Anchor Hull Union Intersect Minkowski Offset .
They start a shape wherever a shape may appear, so they cannot be
definition or part names.
.Pp
Every other keyword is
.Em contextual
and is an ordinary name elsewhere.
Anchor words
.Ql top bot rt lft fwd bak ctr ,
with aliases
.Ql up dn down right left front back center ,
are directions only where a direction is expected: after
.Ql at ,
.Ql on ,
.Ql cutat ,
.Ql anchor ,
.Ql ⚓ ,
.Ql ⌖
and
.Ql ⋈ ,
in
.Ql datum ,
.Ql frame
and
.Ql place ,
in an
.Pa .assemble
.Ql ▽face ,
and as a
.Ql fastener
face.
Stage words
.Pq Ql x y z move at on cut add hull ...
are stages only directly after
.Ql |> .
The
.Pa .assemble
statement words
.Ql plate clearance fastener plan tolerance fixed mate
are statements only at the start of a line, and not when followed by
.Ql =
or
.Ql ← .
So
.Ql top = box 2 2 2
with
.Ql main = base ⌖ top top
is valid: the first
.Ql top
is the anchor, the second the shape.
.Pp
Adding an anchor, stage or statement word never breaks a file.
Adding a primitive shape word is a breaking change, because it must be
reserved; see
.Pa docs/SYNTAX.md .
.Sh ASSEMBLIES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
plate 250 250 6 180 brim=8           // bed width depth [margin [max height]] [brim=N|auto]
clearance 0.15                       // minimum gap for `coscad check`
tolerance 0.00001                    // loop closure: mm [rotation] (defaults 1e-6, 1e-8)
gap = 2                              // numbers work here too
center ← center.coscad ×1
larch  ← l_arch.coscad ×1 ▽top seam=rear
rail   ← rail.coscad ×0              // ×0: premade, assembly-only
corner ← corner.assemble ×2          // recursive subassembly
asm = center ⊕ (larch |> move (55 + gap) -13 -7) ⊕ rail
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql ▽anchor
declares which face goes on the bed; undeclared parts get the automatic
orientation search in
.Cm next .
.Ql key=value
entries are free hints carried into the manifest.
The optional 4th
.Ql plate
argument is the tallest part the printer builds in mm (default 250; 180 for
an A1 mini).
.Cm next
never stands a part on a face taller than that, and exits 2 when a declared
.Ql ▽
or every face is too tall; the design stage only warns.
The packer turns parts 90\(de about Z when that saves a shelf or a plate
(recorded as
.Ql rot_z
in each placement) and fills gaps with later, smaller parts before it opens
another plate.
.Pp
.Ql fixed
poses and the
.Ql roll=
/
.Ql gap=
options of
.Ql mate
take numeric atoms: a literal, a numeric binding,
.Ql -w ,
or
.Ql (w / 2) .
.Ql tolerance mm [rotation]
sets how far a mating loop may miss and still close.
A frame inside a constrained subassembly is addressed through instance
aliases:
.Ql mate l @a s @q.b .
.Ql brim=N
reserves an N mm brim around each instance when packing;
.Ql plate … brim=N
reserves it in
.Cm next
for parts whose printability recommends a brim (small first layer, tall and
narrow);
.Ql brim=auto
reserves 5 mm.
A part's own
.Ql brim=0
opts out.
For
.Cm plan ,
the hints
.Ql profile=2020
(a T-slot rail: four slot faces, ends open unless
.Ql ends=open,blocked ) ,
.Ql material=aluminium|printed|steel
(torque class; printed parts default to printed, premade
.Ql ×0
parts to aluminium),
.Ql torque=Nm ,
and
.Ql mass=g
describe the parts, and one line per screw group describes the joints:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fastener M5x10 br#1 rx#1 bot at=30          // spec clamped host face
fastener M5x10 px ry#2 rt ×2                // two screws along the overlap
fastener M5x10 br1 railA top through=6      // counterbored: 6 mm under the head
fastener M3x10 corner#1 bar#1 top nut=none  // threads into the printed bar
fastener peg strut#1 corner#1 rt            // friction joint, no screw
plan beam=30 rest=bot,top                   // optional search settings
.Ed
.Pp
Instance names take
.Ql #n
when a part is placed more than once.
The part hint
.Ql after=instance[,instance...]
requires those instances to be placed first (for example, before a cap
covers a rotor's entry). Unknown, ambiguous, and cyclic references are errors.
Options:
.Ql ×n ,
.Ql at=mm[,mm]
(from the host's marked end),
.Ql nut=dropin|slidein|hex|none ,
.Ql pocket=face
(hex nuts: the host face the nut pocket opens on;
.Ql pocket=clamped.face
for a pocket in the clamped part),
.Ql head=button|socket ,
.Ql through=mm ,
.Ql inset=mm
(peg joints only: seating plane this far inward from the named host face),
.Ql torque=Nm .
The
.Ql plan
line takes
.Ql beam=N
(search width, default 30),
.Ql rest=faces ,
and the cost weights
.Ql flip vertical driver shrink sibling loose .
.Pp
For a screw located by model frames, replace the host face with
.Ql @hole
and use
.Ql seat=@head_seat
on the clamped part. The hole and head frames must be coaxial, with
matching outward normals. With
.Ql nut=hex ,
.Ql pocket=@nut_seat
names the host nut bearing seat (or
.Ql pocket=clamped.@nut_seat
for a seat in the clamped part), with its normal opposing the head.
Thread reach is computed from the distance between bearing seats.
Frame-based joints take one screw per declaration, without
.Ql at= ,
.Ql through= ,
or
.Ql inset= .
.Pp
The part hint
.Ql preload=hand
loads nuts into the independent part before placing it. The default
.Ql preload=placed
loads nuts after placement and checks access against the bench and other parts.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success and 1 when a command could not run: a usage error, a
missing file, a syntax or geometry error, or an OpenSCAD failure.
It exits 2 when the input is well formed but the design has a problem, so a
pipeline can tell "fix the model" from "fix the setup":
.Cm validate
and
.Cm plan
with design errors (colliding screws, too little thread reach, a clamp off
its host face),
.Cm check
when any overlap is found, and
.Cm next Fl -strict
when any part has a printability warning, and the design stage and
.Cm next
when a part does not fit the plate (its footprint either way round, or, in
.Cm next ,
its height over the plate's max height).
Every command that loads an assembly
.Pq Cm validate , the design stage, Cm next , Cm plan , Cm check , Cm site
also exits 2 when its mating constraints contradict each other (a loop that
does not close, or two fixed roots that disagree), when a part is reached by
no fixed root, or when an instance is fixed twice.
Unknown instances or frames, syntax errors and non-finite values in
constraints still exit 1.
.Cm doctor
exits 1 when OpenSCAD, BOSL2, or the test render fails.
.Pp
Every diagnostic goes to standard error as
.Ql Error: file:line:col: reason ,
optionally followed by a source excerpt with a caret; design errors from
.Cm plan
read
.Ql Error: file.assemble: design: reason .
A constraint conflict is reported at the constraint declared last on the
loop and is followed by one
.Ql "  note: file:line:col: constraint"
line for every constraint on the loop, any of which may be the wrong one;
a floating part is reported at its declaration.
Progress and results go to standard output.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A bracket built relationally, then rendered:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
// bracket.coscad
plate  = box 5 15 20
flange = box 16 15 5
main = plate
  |> at top 5.5 0 -5 flange
  |> cutat lft 3.5 0 0 (xcyl 2.7 12)      // M5 clearance
  |> cutat top 2.5 0 -4 (zcyl 1.4 10)     // M3 pilot

$ coscad stl bracket.coscad
Wrote bracket.scad
Wrote bracket.stl: 664 triangles, volume 2638.931 mm^3, bounds ...
.Ed
.Pp
A funnel from numeric bindings and lofts:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
r_top = 20
r_bot = 8
h = 40
wall = 2
outer = loft 0 (⭘ r_bot) h (⭘ r_top)
inner = loft -1 (⭘ (r_bot - wall)) (h + 1) (⭘ (r_top - wall))
main = outer ⊖ inner
.Ed
.Pp
Generate build instructions for a 2020 frame:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ coscad plan cube.assemble --png
plan: 29 part instances, 38 fasteners on 12 hosts
Wrote cube_plan.md (29 steps, 4 flips, cost 183)
Wrote cube_step1..29.scad and .png
.Ed
.Pp
Manufacture a three-part assembly and check its fits:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ coscad next bow3.assemble
Wrote bow3_bed1.stl (3 parts)
Wrote bow3_manifest.json
$ coscad check bow3.assemble
check: 3 parts, clearance = 0.0mm
contact  center x larch
contact  center x rarch
check: 0 overlaps, 0 clearance warnings
.Ed
.Pp
Verify the toolchain on a new machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ coscad doctor
OpenSCAD    ok       /opt/homebrew/bin/openscad (2025.05.16)
BOSL2       ok       /Users/me/Documents/OpenSCAD/libraries/BOSL2 (2.0.754)
round trip  ok       box 10 10 10 ⊖ zcyl 2 20 -> 874.67 mm^3 (expected 874.67)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Errors carry
.Ql file:line:col
and quote the offending line.
Distinguished cases: syntax errors, undefined names, circular dependencies
(only the cycle is listed), duplicate definitions, a number used as a shape
or a shape as a number, 2D/3D mismatches (extruding a solid, offsetting a
solid, combining a profile with a solid, a 2D
.Ql main ) ,
and invalid loft profiles.
OpenSCAD warnings during a render
.Pq a missing include, a dropped child
are treated as failures.
